#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:130
msgid "Fires right before the BrikPanel simplified editor persists a product. Use it to validate or pre-process submitted data. Runs for both simple and variable products."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:137
msgid "Fires after a product has been fully saved through the BrikPanel editor, including variations. Ideal for syncing to external systems, audit logs, or cache invalidation."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:144
msgid "Register custom cards inside the BrikPanel product editor. Each box renders as a native BrikPanel card at the chosen position (top, middle, bottom) and is sorted by priority."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:151
msgid "Register your own controls in the BrikPanel top bar. Give an item a callback (or just a label and a link) and it renders as a native top bar control. Place it with position (left/right) plus before/after a built-in item, and order it with priority. Valid before/after anchors: brand, live, search, create, notifications, hidden_notices, view_site, custom_link, user. An unrecognised anchor falls back to the default slot instead of erroring. Registered items appear in WooCommerce → Settings → BrikPanel with the same on/off switch and per-role audience rules as the built-in controls, so no extra visibility code is needed; pass 'settings' => false to opt out of that. One registration is all you need: on screens where the BrikPanel top bar stands down (see brikpanel_topbar_is_rendering) the item is placed in the native WordPress admin bar instead, so do NOT also add your own admin_bar_menu node or it will show twice. Pass 'admin_bar' => false if you would rather handle the native bar yourself."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:158
msgid "Not a hook but a public function: whether the BrikPanel top bar is the bar being drawn on this request. It answers correctly from admin_bar_menu onwards, because that hook runs once the current screen is known; an init-time check cannot tell, since the answer depends on the screen. Returns false where the top bar deliberately stands down and the native WordPress admin bar takes over: the block editor (posts, pages, widgets, navigation), the site editor, the customizer, Desktop Mode, Network and User Admin, and users without manage_woocommerce or manage_options. Also false when the store owner switched the top bar off, or turned BrikPanel off for that user. If you registered through brikpanel_topbar_items you do not need this at all, BrikPanel already moves your item to the native bar for you."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:165
msgid "Filter which built-in BrikPanel editor sections are rendered. Useful for hiding sections programmatically (e.g. for specific user roles or product types)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:172
msgid "Filter the full list of BrikPanel settings fields rendered on the WooCommerce → Settings → BrikPanel tab. Lets you append your own WC settings fields (sections, checkboxes, selects…) to the tab."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:179
msgid "Register additional columns in the BrikPanel modern products list. A definition takes a label, a render callback and an optional width (an integer for pixels, or a CSS length such as \"15%\"). Only definitions carrying a render callback are drawn; they appear after the built-in columns and before the row actions, and are listed under \"Plugin columns\" in the Columns picker. The callback receives the product and its ID and returns the cell HTML. For a variable product it receives the parent, so you can aggregate across its variations. Cell output is sanitised the same way third-party column cells are, a callback that echoes instead of returning also works, and a callback that throws leaves an empty cell instead of breaking the list. New columns are visible by default; add \"default\" => false to make them opt-in. These columns are display-only: they cannot be sorted and are not part of CSV exports."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:186
msgid "Meta keys the BrikPanel product list search scans in addition to the post title and content. Defaults to the WooCommerce SKU key _sku. Add a supplier code, manufacturer part number or EAN key and staff can find a product by typing it. Both a product and its variations are scanned, and a match on a variation returns the parent product, so simple and variable products behave the same. Keys that are not plain meta key names are dropped, and at most 10 keys are scanned."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:193
msgid "Terms the Cmd/Ctrl + K order search runs against. The list starts with what the admin typed plus, when that looks like a phone number, every common way the same number is written. Each term is matched against the order number, the billing and shipping phone, the billing email and the billing and shipping customer name. You normally do not need this for phone formats: BrikPanel already compares phone numbers with the separators taken out and matches on the national part, so an order stored as \"+44 7911 123456\" is found by typing 07911123456, 447911123456 or 07911-123456 with no code at all. Reach for this filter when a store has a convention BrikPanel cannot guess, such as a legacy order prefix, an internal customer reference or a second number kept in custom meta. Terms are sanitised, de-duplicated, limited to 100 characters each and capped at 12; a return that is not an array, or one that sanitises down to nothing, falls back to the typed query, so a mistake here can never break the search. $source is \"orders\" today and is reserved for other sections."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:200
msgid "The order IDs the Cmd/Ctrl + K palette found, after every built-in lookup has run and before the orders are loaded and product SKU matches are merged in. Use it to add orders your own index knows about, or to drop orders the current user should not see. Order matters: the list arrives ranked most exact first (order number, then phone, then email, then customer name) and only the first 20 are shown, so put anything that must win at the front. Returned values are cast to whole numbers, de-duplicated and capped; a return that is not an array is ignored. IDs that are not real orders are simply skipped when the orders load. $terms is the same list brikpanel_search_terms produced, so you can reuse it instead of parsing the query again."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:207
msgid "Add, remove or reorder the sections of the Cmd/Ctrl + K command palette. Out of the box there are four: orders, products, customers and navigation. Each source is an array with an id, a label used as the section heading, a capability the current user must have, and a callback that receives the typed query and returns that section's list markup. Sections appear in the order of this array. Built-in sources still obey their on/off switch in WooCommerce → Settings → BrikPanel → Search; a source with an id of your own has no switch and is always on. The capability is checked again before your callback runs, and a source whose callback is not callable is skipped rather than fatal. $context is \"admin\" today and is reserved."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:214
msgid "Tune how many products BrikPanel processes per batch during bulk operations. Lower for shared hosting, higher on beefy servers."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:221
msgid "Fires when a NEW email lands in the abandoned-cart list (typed at checkout, submitted in the signup popup, or auto-captured from a logged-in account). $entry contains email, name, source, status, the decoded cart snapshot and timestamps."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:228
msgid "Fires when a captured cart is marked abandoned (no activity for the configured number of minutes). This is the hook a follow-up email plugin subscribes to. $entry includes the cart snapshot with per-item variation attributes."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:235
msgid "Fires when a captured cart converts into a sale: the matching order (matched by billing email or by the visitor id recorded at checkout) reached a status other than pending, failed, cancelled or draft. It does NOT fire the moment the order is created, so a declined payment never counts. Use it to cancel any queued follow-up emails for that address."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:242
msgid "Fires when a recovery is withdrawn because its order stopped being a sale: the payment failed on a later attempt, the order was cancelled, or it was deleted. The cart goes back to abandoned (or active) and is open for follow-up again, so this is where you re-queue the sequence you cancelled on brikpanel_cart_recovered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:249
msgid "Not a hook but a public query function: fetch captured cart-abandonment entries filtered by status (active|abandoned|recovered), source (checkout|popup|account), search, date range or an updated-since timestamp. Runs the abandonment sweep first, so statuses are always current."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:256
msgid "Decide whether BrikPanel may track the current storefront visitor. The integration point for cookie-consent platforms that do not speak the WordPress Consent API: return false to hold tracking back, true to release it, with no plugin files touched. $context tells you which surface is asking: endpoint (the tracker request), add_to_cart, checkout, or an empty string. Returning true cannot switch tracking back on when the merchant has turned Visitor tracking off entirely, and the filter runs on hot storefront hooks, so keep the callback cheap."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:263
msgid "Not a hook but a public JavaScript API, available on the storefront while \"Wait for cookie consent\" is on. Call brikpanel_start_tracking() the moment the visitor allows analytics and BrikPanel begins tracking immediately, with no page reload. Call brikpanel_stop_tracking() when consent is withdrawn: tracking stops at once and BrikPanel deletes its own cookies and browser storage for that visitor. Both are safe to call more than once. Banners that speak the WordPress Consent API (Complianz, CookieYes, Moove GDPR Cookie Compliance, WPConsent, Cookiebot, iubenda, Beautiful Cookie Consent) need neither, because BrikPanel follows their consent events on its own. Use this for the ones that do not, such as CookieAdmin, Real Cookie Banner and Termly: pair the JavaScript below with the brikpanel_frontend_tracking_allowed filter so the server-side add-to-cart and checkout counters see the same decision."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:270
msgid "Not a hook but a public JavaScript API, available on the storefront while the cart abandonment popup's \"Wait for cookie banner\" setting is on. Call it the moment the visitor answers your cookie banner, whether they accepted or declined, and the signup popup starts its normal delay from that moment instead of opening on top of the banner. Safe to call more than once, and safe to call before the visitor has a cart. Banners that speak the WordPress Consent API (Complianz, CookieYes, Moove GDPR Cookie Compliance, WPConsent, Cookiebot, iubenda, Beautiful Cookie Consent) need nothing here, because BrikPanel follows their consent events on its own. Use this for the ones that do not, such as CookieAdmin, Real Cookie Banner and Termly. The popup opens by itself 30 seconds after the page loads in any case, so a banner that never reports can never cost you a signup."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:277
msgid "Override which SEO plugin BrikPanel surfaces inside the product editor. Return an array with keys slug, label, metabox_ids, or null to disable integration."
msgstr ""

#: includes/brikpanel-hooks-api.php:284
msgid "Which metaboxes open in the right column of the order screen instead of under the \"More\" tab. BrikPanel ships a list of well known invoice, packing slip, shipping label and tracking boxes, so the things a merchant needs on every order stay one glance away. Use this to add a box BrikPanel does not know about — a local courier plugin, or your own — or to remove one it places wrongly for your shop. Pass metabox ids, exactly as the plugin registered them with add_meta_box(). Only short boxes belong here: a wide table or a long form has more room under \"More\". Everyone can still override the result for themselves under Screen Options → \"Show in the sidebar\", and a personal choice always wins over this filter."
msgstr ""

